{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784821338,"nonce":"oAGnbrFI0SWuAhN5SQ5FxPJODrg=","message":"## Follow-up thread (2026-07-23)\n\nMark re-raised this on the list:\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/tools/dda16a07-f0d6-4d20-b6b8-7c5d65e813ec@sirena.org.uk/\n(root: \u003cb356bccf-8e14-41da-8369-53eb442d0f30@sirena.org.uk\u003e)\n\nHe restates the two gaps already analysed here (no list-level 'upgrade\navailable' cue; upgrade action gated out of `waiting`, forcing a manual\n'return to reviewing' first) and adds a new, distinct observation:\n\n\u003e it seems like the ones that don't move out of waiting for new version\n\u003e are also the ones where you have to return to reviewing before it offers\n\u003e you the option of doing the upgrade even though the main list UI tells\n\u003e you the upgrade is available.\n\n## RCA — gap 3: auto-promotion from `waiting` is suppressed for\n## already-recorded revisions\n\nThe auto-upgrade-on-\\[u]pdate path (the one the detail hint promises,\n_tracking_app.py:2052-2055 'will auto-upgrade on update') does not fire\nreliably. Root cause is a mismatch between two different notions of 'a\nnewer version exists':\n\n- **What lights up the UI (`has_newer`).** _tracking_app.py:1030-1032:\n  `has_newer = newest_recorded_revision \u003e current_rev`. Purely a\n  comparison of the newest revision in the DB against the tracked one;\n  it does not care *when* that revision was recorded.\n\n- **What actually triggers promotion.** update_series_tracking records\n  *all* discovered revisions on every update, regardless of status\n  (_review.py:2304-2321), capturing `previously_known` = the full\n  recorded-revision set at cycle start (_review.py:2298-2303). Promotion\n  then runs only if `_should_promote_waiting` is true\n  (_review.py:2333), which is `any(v not in previously_known for v in\n  newer_vers)` (_review.py:38-46) — i.e. it requires a newer revision\n  that has *never before been recorded in the DB*.\n\nThese disagree whenever a newer revision was recorded **before** the\nseries entered `waiting`. Concrete sequence, all ordinary:\n\n1. Series tracked at v1, status `reviewing`. An \\[u]pdate discovers v2\n   and records it (add_revision). `has_newer` is now true.\n2. Maintainer isn't ready, marks the series `waiting` (action_waiting,\n   _tracking_app.py:4477 — sets status only; recorded revisions and\n   current_rev untouched).\n3. Every later \\[u]pdate: `newer_vers=[2]`, but `2` is already in\n   `previously_known`, so `_should_promote_waiting` is false → no\n   promotion. `has_newer` stays true, so the detail panel keeps saying\n   'v2 available, will auto-upgrade on update' and the row still reads as\n   upgradable, but update never promotes.\n\nThe series is stuck in `waiting` with only the manual Review→Upgrade\nroute left — which is precisely the correlation Mark reports.\n\nThe gate is deliberate (docstring _review.py:40-45: stop a broken v2 that\nfails to apply from repeatedly re-waking a re-parked series). The defect\nis that `previously_known` is an all-time, DB-wide set, so it cannot\ndistinguish 'v2 was already tried and rejected' from 'v2 was merely\nrecorded but never acted upon'. There is no per-waiting-episode watermark\nmarking which revisions were already present when the series was parked.\n\n## Related\n\nGaps 1 and 2 unchanged from the RCA above (comment #1). `cc3f07d` is a\nsibling state-machine stickiness bug (`partial` survives a revert).\n`47d5a4c`/`2235355` cover upstream *discovery* of new revisions.\n\nNote on 'the main list UI tells you': the row builder (SeriesRow.compose,\n_tracking_app.py:540-619) still does **not** render `has_newer` (gap 2\nholds). The only list-level cue when a new version lands is the unseen\nfollow-up-message accent in the Msgs column (:616-617), which is\nincidental to the new-thread arrival, not a dedicated upgrade marker.","files":null}]}